\usepackage{booktabs}

\title{Effect of Nitrogen Rate on Yield Components of Pot-Grown
Rice (Pilot Trial)}
\author{Crop Cultivation Group}
\date{July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Objective}
To quantify how three nitrogen application rates affect grain
yield and its components of japonica rice under pot conditions,
and thereby inform the treatment design of the autumn
split-plot field trial.

\section{Materials and Methods}
The test cultivar is the japonica rice Wuyunjing~7.
The pot trial comprises three nitrogen levels:
N0 (0 kg/ha), N90 (90 kg/ha), and N180 (180 kg/ha),
each with four replicates in a completely randomised layout.
Each pot holds 12 kg of sieved paddy soil and is planted with
three hills of two seedlings.
Nitrogen is split as basal : tillering : panicle fertiliser
in a 5:3:2 ratio; phosphorus and potassium are applied once
as basal dressing at identical rates across treatments.
A shallow water layer of 2--3 cm is maintained throughout
the growing season, with conventional pest control.
At maturity, five representative panicles per pot are sampled
to measure grain number per panicle and thousand-grain weight;
the remainder is harvested, sun-dried, and weighed,
and the yield is converted to 14\% moisture content before
being entered in Table~\ref{tab:yield}.

\section{Results}
